    "result_text": "I've implemented `build_context` in `app/context.py`, and all 8 tests pass (the 4 existing ones plus 4 new ones).\n\n```python\ndocs = retriever.invoke(question, config={\"tags\": [TRACE_TAG]})\n```\n\n- **Lookup:** it calls `retriever.invoke`, which every LangChain retriever supports, so it works with both `KeywordRetriever` and a vector-store retriever. The tag is passed in the call's config, so it goes on the retriever's trace run without changing the retriever object.\n- **Entries:** it goes through the documents in the order the retriever returned them and skips any whose `metadata[\"source\"]` has already been used. It stops at `MAX_SOURCES` entries. Each entry is `\"[n] <source>\"` followed by the stripped document text, and entries are joined with one blank line. If nothing is found it returns `\"\"`.\n- **No new dependencies.**\n\n**Tests:** I added `tests/test_context.py`. It checks:\n- the exact output for a real retrieval from the notes;\n- that a repeated source is skipped and the number of entries is capped;\n- that an empty result gives an empty string;\n- that the retriever run is traced with `TRACE_TAG`, using `collect_runs()` from `langchain_core`, which picks up runs the way a real tracer does.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- **Context7 wasn't used.** CLAUDE.md asks for it, but it returned \"monthly quota exceeded\", so I checked the API in the installed `langchain-core` 1.6.5 source instead. There, `invoke(input, config)` is the entry point and it passes `config[\"tags\"]` on to the trace. The older `get_relevant_documents` no longer exists in this version.\n- **Documents without a `\"source\"`** don't cause an error; they show up as `[n] None`. Only the first such document is included, because later ones count as a repeat of that missing source. `load_notes` always sets a source, but if your production vector store might leave it out, tell me how you want those documents handled."
  },
